 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Credit card rates sit far above the cash rate and have barely moved with it. I couldn't get a personal loan rate, so that half of your question is unanswered.\n\n**Credit cards (RBA indicator, advertised rates)**\n\n| Period | Standard card | Low-rate card | Cash rate (monthly average) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jan 2025 | 20.99% | 13.49% | 4.35% |\n| Jan 2026 | 20.99% | 13.49% | 3.60% |\n| Sep 2026 | 21.24% | 13.49% | 4.36% |\n\n- **Gap to the cash rate:** In Sep 2026 the standard card rate was about 16.9 percentage points above the cash rate (21.24% against 4.36%). The low-rate card was about 9.1 points above.\n- **Cash rate moves:** The cash rate fell from 4.35% in Jan 2025 to 3.60% by Sep 2025. It stayed there until Jan 2026, then rose back to about 4.35% by Jun 2026 and was 4.36% in Sep 2026.\n- **Card rate response:** The standard card rate stayed at 20.99% through all of that. It rose 0.25 points to 21.24% in Sep 2026. The low-rate card stayed at 13.49% throughout.\n- **Gap over time:** Because the card rate didn't move, the gap was widest when the cash rate was lowest, at about 17.4 points in late 2025 and early 2026. It has narrowed slightly since the cash rate rose.\n\n**Personal loans:** The RBA's F5 table has no recent data for fixed or variable unsecured personal loan rates, so I can't compare them. The RBA's F6 table of actual rates paid might have them, and I can check it if you want. Note that the figures above are advertised rates, and rates actually paid are usually lower.",
